-- title:
--   Equivariance of Čerednik–Drinfeld evaluation under an intertwining morphism
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $r$ is a prime number and $\mathcal O$ is a characteristic-zero domain, an $\mathcal O$-algebra structure making the characteristic-zero field $K_0$ a fraction field of $\mathcal O$ being fixed. The base hypotheses are: `hdvr`, that $\mathcal O$ is a discrete valuation ring; `hπ`, that $\pi \in \mathcal O$ is irreducible; `hcomplete`, that $\mathcal O$ is adically complete for the ideal $(\pi)$; `hres`, that $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements; and `hunr`, that $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   The coefficient ring for the unramified direction is a characteristic-zero domain $Onr$, an $\mathcal O$-algebra, together with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $Fr$ of $Onr$, subject to: `hOnr_complete`, adic completeness of $Onr$ for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$; `hOnr_max`, maximality of that ideal; `hOnr_alg`, that every $x \in Onr$ satisfies $p(x) \equiv 0 \pmod \pi$ for some monic $p \in \mathcal O[X]$; `hOnr_closed`, that every monic polynomial over $Onr$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$; and `hFr`, that $Fr(x) \equiv x^{r} \pmod \pi$ for all $x \in Onr$.
--
--   A group homomorphism $v\!\det \colon \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) is given, with `hvdet`: $v\!\det(g) = n$ holds exactly when $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$; thus $v\!\det$ is the $\pi$-adic valuation of the determinant.
--
--   The group-theoretic data are a group $G$, a homomorphism $\sigma \colon G \to \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$, a subgroup $\Gamma \le G$ with `hcent`, the existence of $z \in \Gamma$ with $\sigma z$ a scalar matrix $c \cdot 1$ and $v\!\det(\sigma z) = 2$, and `hodd`, the existence of $w \in \Gamma$ with $v\!\det(\sigma w) = 1$; a subgroup $\Gamma' \le G$ is specified by `hΓ'`: $x \in \Gamma'$ if and only if $x \in \Gamma$ and $v\!\det(\sigma x)$ is even. Further, $\rho \colon G \to \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ with `hρ` asserting that $\rho g$ is the class of $\sigma g$; `hdisc`, that for every vertex $v$ of the tree of homothety classes of full lattices in $K_0^2$ the stabiliser of $v$ in the image $\rho(\Gamma')$ is finite; and `hcocpt`, that there is a finite set $S$ of vertices such that every vertex is carried into $S$ by some element of $\rho(\Gamma')$.
--
--   The first curve is a scheme $\mathcal X$ with a proper flat morphism $f \colon \mathcal X \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, together with a family $\Theta$ which, for each $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, maps a pair consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $Onr \to B$ and a Deligne datum over $B$ (an assignment of a submodule of the base change of each full lattice, with invertible quotient, monotone under inclusions of lattices, equivariant for scalar homotheties, and non-degenerate at every prime of $B$) to a $B$-point of $f$, i.e. a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} B \to \mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$. Its hypotheses are: `hΘnat`, naturality in $B$ along $\mathcal O$-algebra maps; `hΘinv`, that $\Theta$ identifies twisted translates by $\Gamma$, where $x'$ is the twisted translate of $x$ by $\sigma\gamma$ when $x'_1 = x_1 \circ Fr^{-v\!\det(\sigma\gamma)}$ and $x'_2$ is the pullback of $x_2$ along $(\sigma\gamma)^{-1}$ in the sense of Deligne data; `hΘfib`, that for every algebraically closed field $k$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\pi$ nilpotent and every $\psi \colon Onr \to k$, the map $\Theta_k(\psi, -)$ is onto the $k$-points of $f$ and two Deligne data have the same image precisely when they are twisted translates of one another by some $\sigma\gamma$, $\gamma \in \Gamma$; and `hΘuniv`, the universal property of $\Theta$: for every scheme $T$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and every family $\rho'$ of maps into the nilpotent-level points of $T$ which is natural in $B$ and invariant under twisted translation by $\Gamma$, there is a family $u$ of maps from the points of $f$ to those of $T$, natural in $B$, with $u \circ \Theta = \rho'$, and any other natural family $u'$ with $u' \circ \Theta = \rho'$ equals $u$ everywhere.
--
--   The second curve is a scheme $\mathcal Y$ with a proper flat $f_Y \colon \mathcal Y \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, a subgroup $\Gamma_Y' \le G$, and a family $\Theta_Y$ of the same shape as $\Theta$ but with values in the points of $f_Y$, assumed only to satisfy naturality `hΘYnat`.
--
--   The analytic frame consists of a field $C$, an algebra over $K_0$, carrying a valuation with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$, complete and algebraically closed; a commutative ring $R$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra and over which $C$ is an algebra, compatibly with $\mathcal O$ and $K_0$; a pseudo-uniformizer $\varpi$, that is an element $\varpi.\varpi \in K_0$ whose image in $C$ has valuation strictly between $0$ and $1$ and such that the valuation of every nonzero element of $K_0$ is squeezed between a power of that valuation and its inverse; and `hF`, the assertion that $(\pi, \varpi, R)$ is an adic frame: $\pi$ is irreducible, $R \to C$ is injective with image the set of elements of valuation $\le 1$, $R$ is $(\pi)$-adically complete, the elements of $K_0$ of valuation $\le 1$ in $C$ are exactly those coming from $\mathcal O$, the image of $K_0$ in $C$ is closed, and $\pi$ and $\varpi.\varpi$ have the same image in $C$. Also given are an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi_0 \colon Onr \to R$; `hrk`, that for $x, y \in C$ with $v(x) < 1$ and $y \neq 0$ some power $v(x)^n$ is $\le v(y)$; `hval`, that every nonzero $\varepsilon \in \Gamma_0$ dominates the valuation of some nonzero element of $C$; `hex`, that every point of the Drinfeld upper half-plane $\Omega_C$, the complement in $C$ of the image of $K_0$, lies in one of the affinoids of $\varpi$; and the assumption that the ring `Omega.HolRingOf ϖ ρ` is a domain. The latter is the ring of functions $\Omega_C \to C$ whose restriction to each affinoid of level $n$ is a uniform limit of a uniformly bounded sequence of pole-free rational functions; the argument $\rho$ carries no further content beyond recording the group action used below.
--
--   The uniformisations are maps $\Phi$ from the set of adic points (compatible systems of Deligne data over the quotients $R/(\pi^{n+1})$) to the $R$-points of $f$, and $\Phi_Y$ likewise into the $R$-points of $f_Y$. The hypothesis `hΦ` has four clauses: reduction of $\Phi x$ modulo $\pi^{n+1}$ equals $\Theta$ applied to the reduction of $\psi_0$ and to the $n$-th component of $x$; $\Phi$ is surjective; $\Phi x = \Phi x'$ holds precisely when $x' = x \cdot \sigma\gamma$ for some $\gamma \in \Gamma'$; and base change to $C$ is injective on $R$-points of $f$ while every $C$-point of $f$ arises from an $R$-point. The hypothesis `hΦY` is the same list for $\Phi_Y$, $\Theta_Y$ and $f_Y$, with $\Gamma_Y'$ in place of $\Gamma'$.
--
--   A morphism $s_C \colon \operatorname{Spec} C \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is fixed, with `hsC` identifying it with the spectrum of $\mathcal O \to K_0 \to C$, and both base changes $\mathcal X_C = \mathcal X \times_{\mathcal O} C$ and $\mathcal Y_C = \mathcal Y \times_{\mathcal O} C$ are assumed integral.
--
--   The evaluation data are a ring homomorphism $e$ from the function field of $\mathcal X_C$ to the subfield of $\operatorname{Frac}(\mathrm{Hol})$ of elements fixed by every $\gamma \in \Gamma'$, where $\mathrm{Hol} =$ `Omega.HolRingOf ϖ ρ`, and the hypothesis `heval`: for every open $U$ of $\mathcal X_C$ containing the generic point and every section $s$ over $U$ there are $a, b \in \mathrm{Hol}$ with $b$ a non-zerodivisor such that the image under $e$ of the germ of $s$ at the generic point is the fraction $a/b$, and moreover, for every adic point $x$, every $z \in \Omega_C$ equal to the coordinate $x.\mathrm{toOmega}$ of $x$, and every $C$-point $q$ of $\mathcal X_C$ whose first projection is the base change of $\Phi x$ to $C$ and whose second projection is the identity, if $q$ factors through $U$ then $b(z)$ times the value in $C$ of the pullback of $s$ along $q$ equals $a(z)$. The data $e_Y$ and `heYval` are the same for $\mathcal Y_C$, $\Phi_Y$ and $\Gamma_Y'$.
--
--   The finiteness hypotheses are `hfin`: for every open $U$ of $\mathcal X_C$ containing the generic point and every level $n$, only finitely many $z$ in the affinoid of level $n$ are coordinates $x.\mathrm{toOmega}$ of an adic point $x$ admitting a $C$-point $q$ over the base change of $\Phi x$, with identity second projection, which does not factor through $U$; and `hfinY`, the same statement for $\mathcal Y_C$ and $\Phi_Y$.
--
--   Finally, an intertwining is given: a morphism $h \colon \mathcal Y \to \mathcal X$ with `hhf` asserting that $h$ followed by $f$ is $f_Y$; an element $g \in G$ with `hg`, $v\!\det(\sigma g) = 1$ (equivalently $\det \sigma g$ is a unit of $\mathcal O$); `hh`, asserting that for every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent and every pair $x, x'$ with $x'$ the twisted translate of $x$ by $\sigma g$, composing the $B$-point $\Theta_Y(x)$ with $h$ gives $\Theta(x')$; and a morphism $h_C \colon \mathcal Y_C \to \mathcal X_C$ on the base changes with `hhC₁`, that $h_C$ followed by the first projection of $\mathcal X_C$ equals the first projection of $\mathcal Y_C$ followed by $h$, and `hhC₂`, that $h_C$ followed by the second projection equals the second projection.
--
--   Under these hypotheses the conclusion is the following single assertion. For every open $U$ of $\mathcal X_C$, every proof that the generic point of $\mathcal X_C$ lies in $U$, every proof that the generic point of $\mathcal Y_C$ lies in $h_C^{-1}U$, and every section $sec$ of the structure sheaf of $\mathcal X_C$ over $U$: the image under $e_Y$ of the germ at the generic point of $\mathcal Y_C$ of the pullback $(h_C^{\ast} sec)$ over $h_C^{-1}U$, regarded as an element of $\operatorname{Frac}(\mathrm{Hol})$, equals $g^{-1}$ acting on the image under $e$ of the germ of $sec$ at the generic point of $\mathcal X_C$, again regarded in $\operatorname{Frac}(\mathrm{Hol})$.
--
--   This is the equivariance statement attached to the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation: an intertwining morphism between two uniformised curves over $\mathcal O$, matching the moduli interpretations up to the twisted action of a single element $g$ with unit determinant valuation, induces on function fields the translation by $g^{-1}$ of the evaluation embeddings into the field of $\Gamma'$-invariant meromorphic functions on the Drinfeld upper half-plane. It is used in the construction of the isomorphism between the function field of the base-changed curve and that invariant field, where the intertwining morphisms are Atkin–Lehner involutions and the degeneracy maps relating a curve to one of raised level.
